      "url": "https://energynow.com/2026/11/election-energy-divisions-emerge-as-democrats-seek-changes-to-bill-on-energy-permits-divisions-emerge/",
      "title": "Senate advances US energy-permitting bill as House Democrats seek changes",
      "content_html": "The U.S. Senate voted 73-25 last week to begin debate on the Bipartisan American Affordability and Jobs Act, a bill to speed up permitting for power plants, transmission lines, mines and other projects. It would also require large data centers to pay more of the grid-upgrade costs linked to their electricity demand. House Democrats are divided over whether to revise it now or wait until their party takes control of Congress in January; President Donald Trump has indicated he would sign the measure.\n\nSome Democrats say they should use next year’s majorities to pursue stronger limits on fossil-fuel development, while others want to amend the bill and preserve its transmission and clean-energy provisions. The Senate could begin considering amendments this week, followed by a final vote. If approved, the measure would go to the House, where Democratic lawmakers have raised objections to provisions affecting environmental protections and tribal sovereignty.",
      "date_published": "2026-11-15T13:02:43+00:00",
      "authors": [
