      "content_html": "<p>The female-led unit in Mexico City has cut gender-based murders from a historic high in 2021. Now, there are hopes to appoint specialist teams and prosecutors nationwide</p><p>Paz Rodríguez woke up about 5am at her home in Milpa Alta, a suburb of Mexico City, on 20 March 2021. She thought about her plans for the day: pick up yellow lilies and cacti from her mother’s house and sell them in the market. Later that evening, they would cook <em>mole</em>, a traditional Mexican dish, together.</p><p>She reached for her phone, which was flooded with texts from her sister, Oliviera Rodríguez. “Mum’s dog is barking a lot,” she had written. “I keep calling and she won’t respond.”</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2026/nov/16/femicide-crisis-mexico-judge-killed-my-mother-one-more-time\">Continue reading...</a>",
